  ar:charCount "1200"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> ;
  ar:excerpt "## Parent\n\n#121 — spec: Expected-set entry resolves its skill files\n\n## What to build\n\nThe entry-level resolution status retires. Every consumer derives failure from the skill files it already reads, so one fact has one failure vocabulary and no code can consult a stale directory-level answer.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The entry no longer exposes a directory-level resolution status or its reason strings; failure is derived from the skill files the entry owns.\n- [ ] Failure messages produced by the development flow, packaging, and validation keep naming the missing location and reason (no wording loss against the previous messages).\n- [ ] The repo compiles and `cargo test --workspace` is green; no consumer reads the retired status (lint/clippy clean).\n- [ ] Behavior is unchanged versus the previous ticket: development policy, packaging strictness, tarball layout, installed layout, lock format, and all entry-state verdicts are identical.\n- [ ] The existing suites serve as the referee and stay green without relaxing any assertion.\n\n## Blocked by\n\n- #123 — Validation consumes enumerated skill files; codex status becomes a projection.\n- #124 — Dev and pack consume skill files; rele" .
ar:CTX_ISSUE_126 ar:forTask "T126" .
ar:CTX_SPEC_121 a ar:ContextSource ;
  ar:clauseId "CTX_SPEC_121" ;
  ar:title "Spec #121" ;
  ar:sourcePath "corpus/issue-121.json" ;
  ar:sourceStart "1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> ;
  ar:sourceEnd "1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> ;
  ar:charCount "1600"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> ;
  ar:excerpt "## Problem Statement\n\nThe Expected set was given one owner in #110, but the Expected-set entry it returns only resolves a *directory*. Which files that directory owes the toolkit — the root `SKILL.md`, one per runtime variant, and the two codex variants that ship `agent.toml` instead — is still per-consumer folklore. The friction is concrete:\n\n- `validation-runner` rebuilds the source layout (`source_dir` joined with `SKILL.md`, then with each variant) and privately owns the codex exemption.\n- `check_codex_status` independently re-walks the autopilot skill directories, then de-dupes against the caller's skill list because it cannot trust what it was given.\n- `validation-runner` also shadows the entry with a flattened skill record, so the validation verdicts and the report are two parallel lists kept in step by pairing — nothing enforces the alignment.\n- The drift has already produced a shipped bug: a resolved entry whose `SKILL.md` was absent vanished from validation's report while the gate still reported PASS (issue #118). #118 fixed one consumer; the interface still cannot say what files an entry owns.\n- The toolkit-setup procedure tells an agent to derive the expected names and the per-variant instruction files itself — a third re-derivation of the same facts, in prose.\n\n## Solution\n\nAn Expected-set entry resolves the **skill files** it owns, and those files carry the failure vocabulary. The entry exposes one list: the root skill file, one per runtime variant, and an `AgentDefinition` for each variant that ships an `agent.toml` instead of a `SKILL.md`. The entry-level re" .
ar:CTX_SPEC_121 ar:whenSpecLinked "true" .
ar:CTX_ADR_0045 a ar:ContextSource ;
  ar:clauseId "CTX_ADR_0045" ;
  ar:title "ADR 0045" ;
  ar:sourcePath "docs/adr/0045-expected-set-entry-resolves-its-skill-files.md" ;
  ar:sourceStart "1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> ;
  ar:sourceEnd "40"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> ;
  ar:charCount "1100"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> ;
  ar:excerpt "# ADR 0045: An Expected-set Entry Resolves Its Skill Files\n\n> Builds on ADR 0044 (single owner for the Expected set) and ADR 0036 (single\n> discoverable router). Sharpens the term recorded as **Skill file** in\n> `CONTEXT.md`.\n\n## Context\n\nADR 0044 made `skill-index` the single owner of Expected-set enumeration, but the\nentry it returns only resolved a *directory* (`source_dir`, `resolution`). Which\nfiles that directory owes the toolkit — the root `SKILL.md`, one per runtime\nvariant, and the two codex variants that ship `agent.toml` instead — stayed\nper-consumer folklore. `validation-runner` rebuilt the layout (`source_dir.join\n(\"SKILL.md\")`, `variant.join(\"SKILL.md\")`) and privately owned the codex\nexemption; `check_codex_status` re-walked `skills/autopilot/*/codex` and de-duped\nagainst the caller's skill list. A resolved entry whose `SKILL.md` was absent\nvanished from validation's report while the gate still said PASS — ticket #118\npatched that consumer, not the interface.\n\n## Decision\n\nAn Expected-set entry resolves the **skill files** it owns, and those files carry\nthe failure voc" .
ar:CTX_ADR_0045 ar:whenSpecLinked "true" .
ar:CTX_GLOSSARY a ar:ContextSource ;
  ar:clauseId "CTX_GLOSSARY" ;
  ar:title "CONTEXT.md — Skill file" ;
  ar:sourcePath "CONTEXT.md" ;
  ar:sourceStart "19"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> ;
  ar:sourceEnd "21"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> ;
  ar:charCount "423"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> ;
  ar:excerpt "**Skill file**:\nOne source file an Expected-set entry owns and the toolkit can act on: the root `SKILL.md`, a runtime variant's `SKILL.md`, or a runtime variant's `agent.toml`. A variant that ships an `agent.toml` instead of a `SKILL.md` is not missing a skill file — the agent definition is its artifact. An entry is a failed entry when any skill file it owns is missing.\n_Avoid_: skill content, entry content, file target" .
